values in the corresponding order: \n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}| & | \\\\ {v}_{1} & {v}_{2} \\\\ | & |\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nLet $[\\begin{aligned}2 & 1 \\\\ -2 & 1\\end{aligned}]$ Then\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{P}^{-1} & ={[\\begin{aligned}2 & 1 \\\\ -2 & 1\\end{aligned}]}^{-1}=\\frac{1}{4}[\\begin{aligned}1 & -1 \\\\ 2 & 2\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, carrying out the necessary matrix multiplication, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}C & =\\frac{1}{4}[\\begin{aligned}2 & 1 \\\\ -2 & 1\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -4\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}1 & -1 \\\\ 2 & 2\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}[\\begin{aligned}2 & -4 \\\\ -2 & -4\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}1 & -1 \\\\ 2 & 2\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}-\\frac{3}{2} & -\\frac{5}{2} \\\\ -\\frac{5}{2} & -\\frac{3}{2}\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535685 false] [17592186124452 1020 #edb/ref 17592186361120 13194139535685 true] [17592186124493 1020 #edb/ref 17592186361113 13194139535685 true] [17592186124963 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045463 13194139535685 true] [17592186124963 1100 "Find a $2 \\cdot 2$ matrix $C$ that has eigenvalues $λ_{1} = 10$ and $λ_{2} = 5$, and corresponding eigenvectors\n\n$v_{1} = \\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix}, v_{2} = \\begin{bmatrix}3 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}$." 13194139535685 false] [17592186124963 1100 "Find a $2\\times 2$ matrix $C$ that has eigenvalues ${\\lambda }_{1}=10$ and ${\\lambda }_{2}=5,$ and corresponding eigenvectors\n\n$$\n{v}_{1}=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}],\\,{v}_{2}=[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}].\n$$" 13194139535685 true] [17592186124963 1145 "Since $B={{v}_{1},{v}_{2}}$ is a basis of ${R}^{2}$ consisting of the eigenvectors of $C,$ then $C$ must be diagonalizable.\n\nSo, we can write $C=PD{P}^{-1},$ where $P$ is a matrix whose columns are the eigenvectors of $C,$ and $D$ is a diagonal matrix whose entries are the eigenvalues in the corresponding order:\n\n$$\nC={{[\\begin{aligned}| & | \\\\ {v}_{1} & {v}_{2} \\\\ | & |\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{P}{{[\\begin{aligned}{\\lambda }_{1} & 0 \\\\ 0 & {\\lambda }_{2}\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{D}{{{[\\begin{aligned}| & | \\\\ {v}_{1} & {v}_{2} \\\\ | & |\\end{aligned}]}^{-1}}_{⏟}}_{{P}^{-1}}\n$$\n\nLet $P=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 3 \\\\ 2 & 1\\end{aligned}].$ Then,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{P}^{-1} & ={[\\begin{aligned}1 & 3 \\\\ 2 & 1\\end{aligned}]}^{-1}=-\\frac{1}{5}[\\begin{aligned}1 & -3 \\\\ -2 & 1\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, carrying out the necessary matrix multiplication, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}C & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & 3 \\\\ 2 & 1\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}10 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 5\\end{aligned}](-\\frac{1}{5}[\\begin{aligned}1 & -3 \\\\ -2 & 1\\end{aligned}]) \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{5}[\\begin{aligned}10 & 15 \\\\ 20 & 5\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}1 & -3 \\\\ -2 & 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}4 & 3 \\\\ -2 & 11\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535685 true] [17592186125001 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045463 13194139535685 true] [17592186125001 1100 "Find $A^{97}$, where\n\n$A = \\begin{bmatrix}-1 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 2\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}-1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}-1 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 2\\end{bmatrix}^{-1}$." 13194139535685 false] [17592186125001 1100 "Find ${A}^{97},$ where\n\n$$\nA=[\\begin{aligned}-1 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 2\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}-1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]{[\\begin{aligned}-1 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 2\\end{aligned}]}^{-1}.\n$$" 13194139535685 true] [17592186125001 1145 "We have been given the following diagonalization of $A.$\n\n$$\nA={{[\\begin{aligned}-1 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 2\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{P}{{[\\begin{aligned}-1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{D}{{{[\\begin{aligned}-1 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 2\\end{aligned}]}^{-1}}_{⏟}}_{{P}^{-1}}\n$$\n\nSo, since $A=PD{P}^{-1}$, we can compute ${A}^{k}$ by using the formula \n\n$$\n{A}^{k}=P{D}^{k}{P}^{-1}.\n$$\n\nFirst, we find the inverse of the matrix $P.$ We get\n\n$$\n{[\\begin{aligned}-1 & 0 \\